1
00:00:00,160 --> 00:00:14,600
Full of Surprises

2
00:00:14,600 --> 00:00:17,540
The mailman just dropped some mail in our box, Grandpa.

3
00:00:17,540 --> 00:00:21,210
Probably a lot of advertising and bills.

4
00:00:21,210 --> 00:00:22,770
Why don't you write to me, Robbie,

5
00:00:22,770 --> 00:00:46,100
so I can get some interesting mail?

6
00:00:46,100 --> 00:00:48,080
You were right, Grandpa.

7
00:00:48,080 --> 00:00:53,750
Advertising, bills, bills, advertising.

8
00:00:53,750 --> 00:00:56,610
Yes. It's just like I said, Robbie.

9
00:00:56,610 --> 00:00:58,190
Nothing interesting.

10
00:00:58,190 --> 00:01:00,380
You won't believe it, Grandpa,

11
00:01:00,380 --> 00:01:02,580
but there's a letter here addressed to you,

12
00:01:02,580 --> 00:01:05,290
Mr. Malcolm Stewart, and it looks like a personal letter.

13
00:01:05,290 --> 00:01:06,830
Oh, it must be a bill.

14
00:01:06,830 --> 00:01:08,270
I don't think so.

15
00:01:08,270 --> 00:01:09,940
The return name and address is

16
00:01:09,940 --> 00:01:13,490
Pete Waters, RFD Number 1, Chesterton.

17
00:01:13,490 --> 00:01:17,240
You're joking. Pete Waters?

18
00:01:17,240 --> 00:01:20,850
Pete Waters, RFD Number 1, Chesterton.

19
00:01:20,850 --> 00:01:22,010
You know him?

20
00:01:22,010 --> 00:01:24,670
Do I know Pete Waters?

21
00:01:24,670 --> 00:01:26,560
You bet I do!

22
00:01:26,560 --> 00:01:33,820
He was my roommate in college.

23
00:01:33,820 --> 00:01:35,970
He visited with Grandma and me in Florida

24
00:01:35,970 --> 00:01:44,610
about five years ago.

25
00:01:44,610 --> 00:01:45,860
What does he say?

26
00:01:45,860 --> 00:01:47,900
Is he OK?

27
00:01:47,900 --> 00:01:52,420
Yeah, he's fine.

28
00:01:52,420 --> 00:01:53,040
Just fine.

29
00:01:53,040 --> 00:01:53,840
He's writing to invite me

30
00:01:53,840 --> 00:01:57,240
to spend a weekend with him at his farm.

31
00:01:57,240 --> 00:01:58,590
He's planning a get-together

32
00:01:58,590 --> 00:02:01,500
with two or three other college friends.

33
00:02:01,500 --> 00:02:06,340
A kind of a fifty-year anniversary reunion.

34
00:02:06,340 --> 00:02:08,480
Sounds like fun.

35
00:02:08,480 --> 00:02:10,710
Fifty years? Wow!

36
00:02:10,710 --> 00:02:13,630
It sounds like fun to me, too, Robbie.

37
00:02:13,630 --> 00:02:15,840
What kind of farm does he have?

38
00:02:15,840 --> 00:02:17,960
I've never been there, Robbie,

39
00:02:17,960 --> 00:02:22,160
but he has chickens and cows and all.

40
00:02:22,160 --> 00:02:25,080
That means fresh eggs and fresh milk.

41
00:02:25,080 --> 00:02:27,150
Does he have a family?

42
00:02:27,150 --> 00:02:29,100
No, he doesn't, Robbie.

43
00:02:29,100 --> 00:02:30,820
He never married.

44
00:02:30,820 --> 00:02:32,850
He's not as lucky as I am

45
00:02:32,850 --> 00:02:36,980
to have a family and grandchildren.

46
00:02:36,980 --> 00:02:38,840
I'm a lucky man.

47
00:02:38,840 --> 00:02:41,320
How come he never got married?

48
00:02:41,320 --> 00:02:43,390
That's a good question, Robbie.

49
00:02:43,390 --> 00:02:45,760
A very good question.

50
00:02:45,760 --> 00:02:47,870
He never married because

51
00:02:47,870 --> 00:02:51,320
the girl he was in love with in college married someone else.

52
00:02:51,320 --> 00:02:53,200
As simple as that.

53
00:02:53,200 --> 00:02:55,250
He never got over it.

54
00:02:55,250 --> 00:02:57,160
He must have loved her very much.

55
00:02:57,160 --> 00:03:00,380
Yes. Very much.

56
00:03:00,380 --> 00:03:02,180
Lillian Winters.

57
00:03:02,180 --> 00:03:03,840
She was in our class.

58
00:03:03,840 --> 00:03:05,390
And what happened?

59
00:03:05,390 --> 00:03:07,880
She was in love with Donald McGrath,

60
00:03:07,880 --> 00:03:10,360
the quarterback on our football team.

61
00:03:10,360 --> 00:03:14,210
Football players are always popular with the ladies.

62
00:03:14,210 --> 00:03:18,170
She liked Pete, and they went to dances together.

63
00:03:18,170 --> 00:03:20,750
But her heart was with Donald.

64
00:03:20,750 --> 00:03:23,100
Did he ever get over it?

65
00:03:23,100 --> 00:03:23,490
No, he never did.

66
00:03:23,490 --> 00:03:26,660
Where is she today?

67
00:03:26,660 --> 00:03:29,000
I don't know.

68
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:31,390
Maybe Lillian will be at the reunion.

69
00:03:31,390 --> 00:03:32,850
You think so?

70
00:03:32,850 --> 00:03:34,780
Pete's full of surprises.

71
00:03:34,780 --> 00:03:37,190
I wish I could go there with you, Grandpa.

72
00:03:37,190 --> 00:03:40,370
What do you think the surprise will be?

73
00:03:40,370 --> 00:03:43,260
With Pete, you never know, Robbie.

74
00:03:43,260 --> 00:03:47,320
Won't it be exciting to see all your college friends there again?

75
00:03:47,320 --> 00:03:49,820
It is already.

76
00:03:49,820 --> 00:03:54,720
I'm kind of excited about going now.

77
00:03:54,720 --> 00:03:56,480
Next weekend ...

78
00:03:56,480 --> 00:03:59,620
sleep over Friday and Saturday night

79
00:03:59,620 --> 00:04:02,460
and come back Sunday.

80
00:04:02,460 --> 00:04:06,300
I can't wait!

81
00:04:06,300 --> 00:04:07,820
Don't you think you ought to call Pete

82
00:04:07,820 --> 00:04:09,310
and tell him you're coming?

83
00:04:09,310 --> 00:04:12,310
You're reading my mind, Robbie.
